--TEST--
`Core\Request`'s refusal names the member that was read exactly, so the two readers of one field -- singular header and plural, verb and `isHead` -- are told apart by their own messages -- `rule:security/request-state-throws-in-an-isolate`, `rule:errors/on-limit`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement asked the other way round: every member shares one refusal *rule*,
// and each has to name itself while stating it. `header` and `headers` are the
// pair where that is worth pinning -- one is the other's name plus a letter, so
// a reader that passed its sibling's name to the shared refusal would produce a
// sentence that is true, well-formed, and about the wrong member.
try {
    Core\Request::header("content-type");
} catch (LogicError $one) {
    echo $one->message, "\n";
}
try {
    Core\Request::headers();
} catch (LogicError $all) {
    echo $all->message, "\n";
}

// The other pair reads one token rather than one field: `method` reports a
// `HEAD` as `Get` and `isHead` is the only member that can still say so, so a
// message naming the wrong one of these sends a handler looking at the verb it
// was already given.
try {
    Core\Request::method();
} catch (LogicError $verb) {
    echo $verb->message, "\n";
}
try {
    Core\Request::isHead();
} catch (LogicError $head) {
    echo $head->message, "\n";
}

// And a program continues past all four: the refusal is a rung of `rule:errors/escalation-ladder`'s
// ladder rather than a fatal, so a handler standing on four reads of one class
// reaches the line after them.
echo "still running\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Request::header(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
Core\Request::headers(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
Core\Request::method(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
Core\Request::isHead(): there is no request here — this program is not answering one, as a CLI program, a scheduled script, a job worker and a test are not. That is refused rather than answered empty, because "no request arrived" and "the request sent nothing" are different facts
still running
